/**
\defgroup distrib_mcp Properized MCP
\brief Continuous real-line distribution whose negative log-density is Zhang's minimax concave penalty on an interval around the location and a properizing tail beyond it, with a normalizing constant in closed form through Dawson's integral.

Defined in `<laplace/distrib/mcp.hpp>` as \ref laplace::distrib::mcp_distrib "mcp_distrib", a class template parametrized by the \ref distrib_properizing_tail "properizing tail" `Tail` whose default is \ref laplace::distrib::student_tail "student_tail" (alternatives are \ref laplace::distrib::gaussian_tail "gaussian_tail" and \ref laplace::distrib::logistic_tail "logistic_tail"). The distribution has three parameters of its own plus the tail's own: four parameters with `gaussian_tail` or `logistic_tail`, and five with `student_tail`. This module gives the log-density and its partial derivatives to order \f$6\f$ in those parameters, the mixed derivatives in the observation and the parameters, the expected Fisher information at order zero (`expected_info()`) and its parameter derivatives to order four (`expected_info_d1()` to `expected_info_d4()`, `expected_info_dk()`), the cumulative functions with their logarithms, the proximal operator in the observation (`prox_y()`), the non-smooth points and starting values; everything about the tails themselves is documented on \ref distrib_properizing_tail "the properizing tails page". Every member that evaluates the law is marked `LAPLACE_HD`, so it runs on the host and inside Kokkos kernels; `start_value()` runs on the host only. Against mpmath references (mpmath), the worst measured error of the log-density and its derivatives is \f$163\f$ ulp of the scale of their order, that of the logarithms of the cumulative functions is \f$2\f$ ulp of \f$\max(\lvert \log F \rvert, 1)\f$ (\f$2.46\f$ ulp with `gaussian_tail`), and that of the expected information at order zero is \f$58.7\f$ ulp of its largest component (orders one to four agree within \f$1.29 \times 10^{-9}\f$ relative against five-point differences, and orders one and two within \f$1.05 \times 10^{-15}\f$ relative against the generic quadrature); the measures are those of \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured", and the figures are in the section \"Accuracy and limits\" below.

## Classic form and parametrization

The minimax concave penalty of Zhang (Zhang, 2010) is a function of \f$u = |y - \mu|\f$, the distance of an observation \f$y \in \mathbb{R}\f$ from a location \f$\mu \in \mathbb{R}\f$. It has a level \f$\lambda > 0\f$ and a concavity \f$\gamma > 0\f$, and it is written in two pieces,
\f[
\rho(u) = \begin{cases} \lambda u - \dfrac{u^2}{2\gamma}, & u \le \gamma\lambda, \\[4pt] \dfrac{\gamma\lambda^2}{2}, & u > \gamma\lambda. \end{cases}
\f]
The knot \f$u = \gamma\lambda\f$ separates the two pieces. Beyond the knot \f$\rho\f$ is flat, so \f$e^{-\rho(u)}\f$ has an infinite integral for every value of the parameters. The law keeps \f$\rho\f$ on the inner piece and replaces the flat piece by the exponent \f$H(u - \gamma\lambda)\f$ of a properizing tail (\ref distrib_properizing_tail "Properizing tails"):
\f[
q(u) = \begin{cases} \lambda u - \dfrac{u^2}{2\gamma}, & u \le \gamma\lambda, \\[4pt] \dfrac{\gamma\lambda^2}{2} + H(u - \gamma\lambda), & u > \gamma\lambda, \end{cases}
\f]
and the density is
\f[
f(y) = \frac{e^{-q(|y - \mu|)}}{Z}, \qquad y \in \mathbb{R},
\f]
with \f$Z\f$ the normalizing constant. The log-density is therefore \f$\log f(y) = -q(|y - \mu|) - \log Z\f$. The definition of \f$H\f$ and the parameters of each tail are given on \ref distrib_properizing_tail "the properizing tails page".

The parameters are readable from \f$q\f$. The location \f$\mu\f$ is the mode and the median of the law, and the density is symmetric about it, so its mean is \f$\mu\f$ as well. The rate \f$\lambda\f$ is the slope of \f$q\f$ at the location, because \f$q'(0^+) = \lambda\f$, and the concavity \f$\gamma\f$ places the knot at \f$\gamma\lambda\f$. On the inner piece the exponent of the density is the penalty \f$\rho\f$ of Zhang, which is what gives the parameters their meaning. The SCAD law of \ref distrib_scad "the Properized SCAD page" has the same property with the penalty of Fan and Li (Fan and Li, 2001).

## Density and normalizing constant

The normalizing constant is
\f[
Z = 2\left[\sqrt{2\gamma}\,\mathcal{D}\!\left(\lambda\sqrt{\gamma/2}\right) + e^{-\gamma\lambda^2/2}\,T\right],
\f]
where \f$\mathcal{D}\f$ is Dawson's integral, \ref laplace::math::dawson "dawson()" on \ref dawson "its own page", and \f$T = \int_0^\infty e^{-H(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x\f$ is the tail's constant (\ref distrib_properizing_tail "Properizing tails").

Each piece of \f$q\f$ is polynomial or defined by the tail in \f$u\f$. On the inner piece the exponent \f$-q\f$ is convex in \f$u\f$, so completing the square turns the inner integral into an integral of \f$e^{s^2}\f$, and that integral is Dawson's integral. Beyond the knot, the change of variables \f$x = u - \gamma\lambda\f$ contributes the term \f$e^{-\gamma\lambda^2/2}\,T\f$. Both terms of \f$Z\f$ are positive, so nothing cancels in the sum. The implementation adds the two terms in logarithms, which keeps the tail term when \f$e^{-\gamma\lambda^2/2}\f$ underflows.

## Shape of the log-density

The function \f$q\f$ and its first derivative are continuous at the knot, where \f$q'\f$ vanishes on both sides. The second derivative jumps there from \f$-1/\gamma\f$ to \f$H''(0)\f$, so the density is \f$C^1\f$ in \f$y\f$ at the knot. At \f$u = 0\f$ the derivative \f$q'\f$ jumps from \f$-\lambda\f$ to \f$\lambda\f$, so the log-density has a kink at the location and is \f$C^0\f$ there. The field `y_regularity` is therefore 0.

Two limits hold for every tail. When \f$\gamma\f$ tends to zero the inner piece disappears, and the density becomes \f$e^{-H(|y - \mu|)}/(2T)\f$; for `gaussian_tail` this is the normal density with mean \f$\mu\f$ and variance \f$V\f$ (\ref distrib_gaussian2 "gaussian2"). The test takes \f$\gamma = 10^{-14}\f$ with `gaussian_tail` and compares the log-density with the Gaussian one.

The second limit concerns the Laplace density \f$f_{\mathrm{L}}(y) = \tfrac{\lambda}{2} e^{-\lambda|y - \mu|}\f$ of rate \f$\lambda\f$ (\ref distrib_laplace2 "laplace2"). Let \f$x = \lambda\sqrt{\gamma/2}\f$. When the last knot is sent to infinity so that \f$x\f$ is large, the tail carries no mass and the density approaches \f$f_{\mathrm{L}}\f$. For `gaussian_tail`, the two log-densities differ exactly by
\f[
\log f(y) - \log f_{\mathrm{L}}(y) = \frac{u^2}{2\gamma} - \log\frac{\lambda Z}{2},
\f]
and \f$\log(\lambda Z/2) = 1/(2x^2) + O(x^{-4})\f$, which is the asymptotic expansion of Dawson's integral. The test checks the identity to within \f$10^{-13}\f$ for \f$0 \le u \le 0.1\f$ at \f$\lambda = 30\f$ and \f$\lambda = 300\f$, with \f$\gamma = 3\f$ and \f$V = 1\f$, and it checks the gap \f$\log(\lambda Z/2)\f$ against \f$1/(2x^2)\f$ to within \f$4/x^4\f$.

## Derivatives

The observation and the location enter through \f$u\f$ only, with \f$\partial u / \partial y = \operatorname{sign}(y - \mu)\f$ and \f$\partial u / \partial \mu = -\operatorname{sign}(y - \mu)\f$. Let \f$i\f$ be the order of differentiation in \f$\mu\f$, \f$J\f$ the order in \f$y\f$, \f$(b, m)\f$ the orders in \f$(\lambda, \gamma)\f$, and \f$n\f$ the multi-index of orders in the tail's own parameters \f$\theta_t\f$. Each component of the derivative of \f$\log f\f$ is then
\f[
-(-1)^{i}\,\operatorname{sign}(y - \mu)^{\,i+J}\,\partial_u^{\,i+J}\,\partial_\lambda^{\,b}\,\partial_\gamma^{\,m}\,\partial_{\theta_t}^{\,n}\,q(u)
\;-\;[\,i + J = 0\,]\,\partial_\lambda^{\,b}\,\partial_\gamma^{\,m}\,\partial_{\theta_t}^{\,n}\,\log Z,
\f]
where \f$[\,\cdot\,]\f$ is 1 when its statement holds and 0 otherwise. The power \f$\operatorname{sign}(y - \mu)^{t}\f$ is 1 for even \f$t\f$ and \f$\operatorname{sign}(y - \mu)\f$ for odd \f$t\f$, with \f$\operatorname{sign}(0) = 0\f$.

At \f$y = \mu\f$ the odd orders in \f$\mu\f$ are zero, which is the mean of the two one-sided values. The even orders agree on the two sides and are taken from the inner piece. At a knot the value and every derivative come from the inner piece. Because \f$q\f$ is quadratic in \f$u\f$ on the inner piece, every component with \f$i + J \ge 3\f$ vanishes there; in the tail, the mixed partials of \f$q\f$ are formed by the chain rules through the knot and the tail's parameters (\ref distrib_properizing_tail "Properizing tails").

The mixed partials of \f$q\f$ are closed forms. They were derived with SymPy (Meurer and others, 2017) in `tools/reference/distrib_mcp_scad_forms.py` and `tools/reference/properized_tail_forms.py`, which compare them with SymPy's own derivatives for every order up to six at points inside each piece.

The derivatives of \f$\log Z\f$ are built by recursions, not written out. The tail's parameters enter \f$Z\f$ only through \f$T(\theta_t)\f$, so
\f[
Z = 2\left[A(\lambda, \gamma) + B(\lambda, \gamma)\,T(\theta_t)\right],
\f]
and the table of \f$Z\f$ over all non-location parameters comes from two tables over \f$(\lambda, \gamma)\f$ and from the mixed partials of \f$T\f$ (\ref distrib_properizing_tail "Properizing tails"). The tables over \f$(\lambda, \gamma)\f$ come from the exponential and from Dawson's integral by the chain rule. The table of \f$\log Z\f$ then comes from the logarithm of the table of \f$Z\f$. Three recursions do this work. Each binomial coefficient \f$\binom{\alpha'}{\beta}\f$ below is a product over the variables. In each recursion \f$\alpha\f$ is a multi-index, \f$r\f$ is the first index with \f$\alpha_r > 0\f$, and \f$\alpha' = \alpha - e_r\f$ with \f$e_r\f$ the unit multi-index in direction \f$r\f$. The letter \f$\gamma\f$ is the concavity throughout this page and never a multi-index.

The table of \f$e^{\varphi}\f$ comes from the table of \f$\varphi\f$:
\f[
\partial^{\alpha} e^{\varphi} = \sum_{\beta \le \alpha'} \binom{\alpha'}{\beta}\, \partial^{\beta} e^{\varphi}\; \partial^{\alpha' - \beta + e_r} \varphi.
\f]

The table of \f$f^{(k)}(x)\f$, the \f$k\f$-th derivative of a function \f$f\f$ at a point \f$x\f$ that is a function of the parameters, is built downward from \f$k\f$ equal to the top order:
\f[
\partial^{\alpha} f^{(k)}(x) = \sum_{\beta \le \alpha'} \binom{\alpha'}{\beta}\, \partial^{\beta} f^{(k+1)}(x)\; \partial^{\alpha' - \beta + e_r} x.
\f]

The table of \f$\log Z\f$ comes from the table of \f$Z\f$, with \f$Z_{\mathbf{0}}\f$ the value of the table at the point:
\f[
\partial^{\alpha} \log Z = \frac{1}{Z_{\mathbf{0}}}\Big(\partial^{\alpha} Z - \sum_{\mathbf{0} \ne \beta \le \alpha'} \binom{\alpha'}{\beta}\, \partial^{\beta} Z\; \partial^{\alpha' - \beta + e_r} \log Z\Big).
\f]

The first two recursions act on tables over \f$(\lambda, \gamma)\f$. The last acts on a triangularly packed table over \f$(\lambda, \gamma, \theta_1)\f$ for a one-parameter tail, holding 84 entries against 343 for a cube at order 6, or over \f$(\lambda, \gamma, s, \nu)\f$ for `student_tail`, where the four-variable logarithm recursion holds 210 entries against 2401 for a four-dimensional box (\ref distrib_properizing_tail "Properizing tails").

## Cumulative functions

Let \f$U(u) = \int_u^\infty e^{-q(t)}\,\mathrm{d}t\f$ for \f$u \ge 0\f$, the integral of the unnormalized density beyond the distance \f$u\f$. With \f$T = \int_0^\infty e^{-H(v)}\,\mathrm{d}v\f$ and \f$G(x) = \int_x^\infty e^{-H(v)}\,\mathrm{d}v\f$ the tail's constant and partial integral (\ref distrib_properizing_tail "Properizing tails"),
\f[
U(u) = \begin{cases} \sqrt{2\gamma}\; e^{-q(u)}\,\mathcal{D}\!\left(\dfrac{\gamma\lambda - u}{\sqrt{2\gamma}}\right) + e^{-\gamma\lambda^2/2}\,T, & u \le \gamma\lambda, \\[8pt] e^{-\gamma\lambda^2/2}\,G(u - \gamma\lambda), & u > \gamma\lambda. \end{cases}
\f]
The cumulative distribution function is \f$F(y) = U(|y - \mu|)/Z\f$ for \f$y \le \mu\f$ and \f$F(y) = 1 - U(|y - \mu|)/Z\f$ for \f$y > \mu\f$. Every term of \f$U\f$ is a product of positive factors, so no difference is taken between two nearly equal numbers.

The functions `log_cdf()` and `log_ccdf()` compute the logarithm of the side whose probability is small, which can fall below the smallest positive double. They compute the other side as one minus a plain ratio. A logarithm of the small side would carry the rounding error of its own exponent into the result, one ulp per unit of the size of \f$\log F\f$.

The derivatives of \f$\log F\f$ in the parameters and the quantile are not members of this law: the \ref distrib_generic "generic routes" compute them.

## Proximal operator

The member `prox_y(v, t)` returns the proximal operator in \f$y\f$ for a point \f$v\f$ and a step \f$t > 0\f$, defined on \ref penalty_prox "the proximal operators page" as the global minimizer of \f$-\log f(y) + (y - v)^2/(2t)\f$. When \f$v = \mu\f$, \f$v\f$ is not finite, or \f$v\f$ is NaN, it returns \f$v\f$ directly. With \f$U = |v - \mu|\f$ and \f$x = |y - \mu|\f$, the normalizing constant \f$\log Z\f$ drops out and the problem reduces to
\f[
\min_{x \ge 0} g(x) = t\,q(x) + \tfrac{1}{2}(x - U)^2.
\f]
Because \f$q\f$ does not decrease on \f$[0, \infty)\f$, the minimizer \f$y^*\f$ carries the sign of \f$v - \mu\f$ and satisfies \f$|y^* - \mu| \le U\f$.

The objective \f$g\f$ is smooth on each piece of \f$q\f$ and grows at infinity, so its global minimum sits at \f$x = 0\f$, at the knot \f$K = \gamma\lambda\f$, or at a stationary point of one piece. The implementation enumerates exactly these candidates, evaluates \f$g\f$ at each, and keeps the one with the least value, letting the smallest \f$x\f$ win any tie. That tie rule matches the convention already used for the nonconvex bridge penalty on \ref penalty_prox "the proximal operators page", and it matters here because \f$q\f$ is nonconvex.

On the inner piece \f$0 \le x \le K = \gamma\lambda\f$, \f$g\f$ is a quadratic with curvature \f$1 - t/\gamma\f$. When \f$1 - t/\gamma > 0\f$ (that is, \f$t < \gamma\f$), its stationary point is the firm threshold
\f[
x = \frac{U - t\lambda}{1 - t/\gamma},
\f]
which is offered as a candidate only when it falls strictly inside \f$(0, K)\f$. When \f$t \ge \gamma\f$, the curvature is at most zero, so there is no interior minimum on this piece and only its ends \f$0\f$ and \f$K\f$ compete. At \f$t = \gamma\f$ with \f$U = t\lambda\f$, \f$g\f$ is flat over the entire inner piece: every point of \f$[0, K]\f$ minimizes \f$g\f$, and the smallest-minimizer rule makes the returned value \f$\mu\f$ well defined.

Beyond the knot \f$K\f$, the positive stationary points of \f$g\f$ come from `tail.prox_roots(U - K, t, z)`, which adds \f$K + z_j\f$ to the candidate list. The stationary equation for each tail, its root count, its asymptotic shrinkage, and why `student_tail` is the default for the proximal operator are explained on \ref distrib_properizing_tail "the properizing tails page".

## Expected information

The member `expected_info()` returns the whole expected Fisher information matrix \f$I(\theta)\f$ at order zero in closed form, in the parameters \f$(\mu, \lambda, \gamma, \theta_t)\f$, where \f$\theta_t\f$ denotes the tail's own parameters. The matrix has three blocks: the location entry \f$I_{\mu\mu}\f$, the cross-entries \f$I_{\mu\theta}\f$ between the location and the other parameters, and the smooth block \f$I_{ab}\f$ over \f$(\lambda, \gamma, \theta_t)\f$.

For the location, \f$-\mathbb{E}[l_{\mu\mu}]\f$ misses the point mass that the kink of \f$q\f$ at \f$y = \mu\f$ puts in the second derivative, so the implementation uses the score form as on \ref distrib_enet "the elastic-net page". One integration by parts turns it into
\f[
I_{\mu\mu} = \mathbb{E}[q'(u)^2]
  = \frac{2}{Z}\left[\lambda + \int_0^\infty q''(u)\,e^{-q(u)}\,\mathrm{d}u\right],
\f]
where \f$q''\f$ is the almost-everywhere second derivative of \f$q\f$ and \f$\lambda\f$ is the jump of \f$q'\f$ at the location. Inside the knot \f$q''(u) = -1/\gamma\f$, and beyond it \f$q''(u) = H''(u - \gamma\lambda)\f$. For every other parameter \f$\theta\f$, \f$I_{\mu\theta} = 0\f$ because \f$l_\mu\f$ is odd and \f$l_\theta\f$ is even in \f$y - \mu\f$; these components are returned as exact zeros.

The smooth block uses the Hessian form,
\f[
I_{ab} = \mathbb{E}[\partial^2_{ab} q] + \partial^2_{ab}\log Z,
\f]
valid because \f$\lambda\f$ and \f$\gamma\f$ have regularity \f$1\f$ (their knot in \f$y\f$ moves with them, so two derivatives are admissible) and the tail parameters are smooth. The derivatives of \f$\log Z\f$ come from the order-two table described in the section "Derivatives" above. The expectations \f$\mathbb{E}[\partial^2_{ab} q]\f$ are closed: inside the knot \f$q\f$ is a quadratic in \f$u\f$ whose coefficients are rational in \f$(\lambda, \gamma)\f$, and beyond the knot every second derivative of \f$q\f$ is a combination of \f$1\f$, \f$H'\f$, \f$H''\f$, and the derivatives of \f$H\f$ in \f$\theta_t\f$. With \f$E = e^{-\gamma\lambda^2/2}\f$, \f$\mathcal{I}_{xx} = \int_0^\infty H''(x)\,e^{-H(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x\f$, \f$\mathcal{I}_{xt,j} = \int_0^\infty \partial_x\partial_{\theta_{t,j}} H(x)\,e^{-H(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x\f$, and \f$\mathcal{I}_{tt,ij} = \int_0^\infty \partial_{\theta_{t,i}}\partial_{\theta_{t,j}} H(x)\,e^{-H(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x\f$ from the tail, the location entry is
\f[
I_{\mu\mu} = \frac{2}{Z}\left[\lambda - \frac{N_0}{\gamma} + E\,\mathcal{I}_{xx}\right],
\f]
and the unnormalized integrals \f$\int_0^\infty \partial^2_{ab} q\,e^{-q}\,\mathrm{d}u\f$ are \f$E(\gamma T + \gamma^2\mathcal{I}_{xx})\f$ for \f$(\lambda, \lambda)\f$, \f$E(\lambda T - 1 + \gamma\lambda\mathcal{I}_{xx})\f$ for \f$(\lambda, \gamma)\f$, \f$-N_2/\gamma^3 + E\lambda^2\mathcal{I}_{xx}\f$ for \f$(\gamma, \gamma)\f$, \f$-\gamma E\,\mathcal{I}_{xt,j}\f$ for \f$(\lambda, \theta_{t,j})\f$, \f$-\lambda E\,\mathcal{I}_{xt,j}\f$ for \f$(\gamma, \theta_{t,j})\f$, and \f$E\,\mathcal{I}_{tt,ij}\f$ for \f$(\theta_{t,i}, \theta_{t,j})\f$. Here \f$N_0\f$ and \f$N_2\f$ are the inner moments below, and the identity \f$\int_0^\infty H'(x)\,e^{-H(x)}\,\mathrm{d}x = 1\f$ supplies the \f$-1\f$ term.

Both this law and \ref distrib_scad "the Properized SCAD law" have one quadratic piece whose exponent's derivative vanishes at its upper end, in \f$u\f$ here and in \f$u - \lambda\f$ for the SCAD. With \f$p = \gamma\f$ here and \f$p = a - 1\f$ for the SCAD, the three moments
\f[
N_k = \int_0^{p\lambda} t^k \exp\!\left(-\lambda t + \frac{t^2}{2p}\right)\mathrm{d}t, \qquad k = 0, 1, 2,
\f]
carry every inner contribution. In the shared implementation `dawson_moments` in `detail/properized_nonconvex.hpp`, \f$N_0 = \sqrt{2p}\,\mathcal{D}(x)\f$ with \f$x = \lambda\sqrt{p/2}\f$, the Dawson value the normalizing constant already holds; \f$\mathcal{D}\f$ is \ref dawson "Dawson's integral", written so on this page because \f$F\f$ is its cumulative function. Differentiating \f$N_0\f$ in \f$\lambda\f$, whose upper end moves with it, gives for \f$w = p\lambda^2 = 2x^2\f$ and \f$E = e^{-w/2}\f$,
\f[
N_1 = p\,[E - \mathcal{D}'(x)], \qquad N_2 = 2p^2\lambda E + p\sqrt{p/2}\,\mathcal{D}''(x).
\f]
Every term is positive, so nothing cancels however large \f$w\f$ grows. By contrast, the plain forms \f$\lambda p N_0 - p(1 - E)\f$ and \f$(p^2\lambda^2 - p)N_0 + p^2\lambda(2E - 1)\f$ lose about one digit per decade of \f$w\f$, reaching \f$7.2 \times 10^{-5}\f$ relative error at \f$w = 10^6\f$ against mpmath (mpmath).

Below \f$w = 1\f$, those plain forms cancel the other way, by \f$\varepsilon/w\f$, so `dawson_moments` switches for \f$N_1\f$ and \f$N_2\f$ to the double series
\f[
N_k = (p\lambda)^{k+1} \sum_{m,n \ge 0} \frac{(w/2)^m (-w)^n}{m!\,n!\,(k + 2m + n + 1)},
\f]
which comes from expanding both exponentials on \f$t = p\lambda s\f$, \f$s \in (0, 1)\f$. Measured against mpmath over \f$w\f$ from \f$10^{-6}\f$ to \f$10^6\f$, the series has relative error \f$10^{-16}\f$ to \f$5 \times 10^{-16}\f$ below \f$w = 1\f$ and fails above (\f$8 \times 10^{-7}\f$ at \f$w = 5\f$), whereas the Dawson form has relative error \f$10^{-16}\f$ to \f$1.6 \times 10^{-15}\f$ above \f$w = 1\f$ and \f$1.5 \times 10^{-9}\f$ at \f$w = 10^{-6}\f$.

Beyond the knot, the three integrals \f$\mathcal{I}_{xx}\f$, \f$\mathcal{I}_{xt,j}\f$, and \f$\mathcal{I}_{tt,ij}\f$ reduce to the tail constant \f$T\f$, to \f$S_0 = T/\varsigma\f$, and to the three moments \f$P_0, P_1, P_2\f$ supplied by `psi_curvature_moment()` on \ref distrib_properizing_tail "the properizing tails page". No new special function is evaluated anywhere in the matrix: the entire closed form reads only Dawson's integral with its first two derivatives, the tail's own constant, and rational arithmetic.

For \f$K = 1, 2, 3, 4\f$, `expected_info_dk<K>()` returns the derivatives of total order \f$K\f$ of every component of \f$I(\theta)\f$ in the parameters, in closed form, for every properizing tail. Convenience members `expected_info_d1()` to `expected_info_d4()` call `expected_info_dk<K>()`. The return value pairs each of the \f$\binom{P+1}{2}\f$ matrix entries with the symmetric tensor of its order-\f$K\f$ derivatives over the \f$P\f$ parameters, where \f$P = 4\f$ for `gaussian_tail` or `logistic_tail` and \f$P = 5\f$ for `student_tail`. Together with `expected_info()`, the law evaluates the expected information in closed form at every order from zero to four.

Because this law is a location law over the whole line, \f$I(\theta)\f$ is a function of \f$(\lambda, \gamma, \theta_t)\f$ alone and does not depend on \f$\mu\f$. Every derivative component whose multi-index contains \f$\mu\f$ is therefore returned as an exact zero, as are \f$I_{\mu\theta}\f$ and all its derivatives. For a five-parameter law, one in five multi-indices contains \f$\mu\f$ at order one and one in two at order four. The generic route cannot serve these components because its regularity rule returns NaN when a derivative is taken in the location, which moves the kink (\ref distrib_generic "Generic routes").

With \f$p = \gamma\f$, every factor of the order-zero closed form depends either on \f$(\lambda, p)\f$ or on the tail's parameters \f$\theta_t\f$, and never on both. No table over all parameters is built at once: each factor carries a table of total order \f$K\f$ over \f$(\lambda, p)\f$ or over \f$\theta_t\f$, and products of one of each are outer products of the two tables. Sums, products, one reciprocal over the joint indices for the division by \f$Z\f$, and the order-\f$(K+2)\f$ table of \f$\log Z\f$ from the section "Derivatives" above assemble every entry. So order four costs $18$ microseconds a call with `gaussian_tail` and $108$ with `student_tail`, measured on one Serial thread in `benchmarks/distrib_generic.cpp`.

The inner moments \f$N_0, N_1, N_2\f$ are needed with their derivatives in \f$(\lambda, p)\f$ to order \f$K\f$, following the same two branches as at order zero. Above \f$w = p\lambda^2 = 1\f$ they come from the same composition of Dawson's function as \f$Z\f$, and below it from the double series in \f$w\f$, which is a polynomial in \f$\lambda\f$ and \f$p\f$ and exact term by term. The derivatives in \f$\theta_t\f$ sit in the tail's three curvature integrals and in \f$T\f$, tabulated at once by `const_table()` (\ref distrib_properizing_tail "Properizing tails"). For `gaussian_tail` and `logistic_tail`, the moments \f$P_0, P_1, P_2\f$ are constants, so those derivatives are monomials in the tail's parameter; for `student_tail`, they are \f$S_0\f$ times rational functions of \f$\nu\f$.

## Non-smooth points

`y_kinks()` declares three points, \f$\mu - \gamma\lambda\f$, \f$\mu\f$ and \f$\mu + \gamma\lambda\f$, each with `continuous_order` 0. The two knots are not kinks, since the second derivative of the density jumps there and the first does not. They are declared so that a quadrature which takes the declared points as cuts has a panel end at every knot.

`param_kinks(j, y)` gives, for each parameter, the points of its own axis where the log-density is not smooth. For \f$\mu\f$ the point is \f$\mu = y\f$, with order 0. For \f$\lambda\f$, when \f$u = |y - \mu| > 0\f$, the point is \f$\lambda = u/\gamma\f$, the value at which the knot reaches \f$u\f$, with order 1. For \f$\gamma\f$, when \f$u > 0\f$, the point is \f$\gamma = u/\lambda\f$, also with order 1. The tail's parameters have no points, because every tail is smooth in its parameters.

## Accuracy and limits

The table lists the worst errors measured against mpmath references (mpmath), computed at 300 and 400 bits (`tools/reference/distrib_mcp_scad.py`). The test loops cover both the properized MCP and SCAD laws, so each figure is the worst over both laws (`tests/distrib/test_mcp_scad_tails.cpp` for `student_tail` and `logistic_tail` over 74184 values, and `tests/distrib/test_mcp_scad.cpp` for `gaussian_tail`). The reference checks run on the host in both the gcc and CUDA (RTX 4070 SUPER) builds, which therefore give the same figures. The method of measurement is described on \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured". The worst point at order 6 is the SCAD with `logistic_tail` at \f$\lambda = 3\f$, \f$a = 1.1\f$ and \f$s = 0.5\f$.

| Quantity | Student and logistic, measured | Student and logistic, bound | Gaussian, measured | Gaussian, bound |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Log-density, order 0 | \f$2\f$ ulp | \f$4\f$ ulp | \f$3.66\f$ ulp | \f$6\f$ ulp |
| Log-density, order 1 | \f$6\f$ ulp | \f$10\f$ ulp | \f$8\f$ ulp | \f$18\f$ ulp |
| Log-density, order 2 | \f$6\f$ ulp | \f$10\f$ ulp | \f$6.3\f$ ulp | \f$18\f$ ulp |
| Log-density, order 3 | \f$10\f$ ulp | \f$16\f$ ulp | \f$4.45\f$ ulp | \f$14\f$ ulp |
| Log-density, order 4 | \f$9\f$ ulp | \f$16\f$ ulp | \f$11.2\f$ ulp | \f$18\f$ ulp |
| Log-density, order 5 | \f$25\f$ ulp | \f$40\f$ ulp | \f$40.2\f$ ulp | \f$64\f$ ulp |
| Log-density, order 6 | \f$163\f$ ulp | \f$250\f$ ulp | \f$163\f$ ulp | \f$250\f$ ulp |
| \f$\log F\f$ and \f$\log(1 - F)\f$ | \f$2\f$ ulp of \f$\max(\lvert \log F \rvert, 1)\f$ | \f$4\f$ ulp | \f$2.46\f$ ulp of \f$\max(\lvert \log F \rvert, 1)\f$ | \f$4\f$ ulp |
| Normalizing constant \f$Z\f$ | \f$2\f$ ulp | \f$3\f$ ulp | \f$1.33\f$ ulp | \f$2\f$ ulp |
| \f$F\f$ and \f$1 - F\f$ or increment of \f$F\f$ against quadrature | \f$8.7 \times 10^{-17}\f$ | \f$10^{-10}\f$ relative | \f$5.7 \times 10^{-14}\f$ relative | \f$10^{-11}\f$ relative |
| Mass of the density, every knot declared as a cut | \f$3.3 \times 10^{-16}\f$ | \f$10^{-13}\f$ | \f$4.4 \times 10^{-16}\f$ | \f$10^{-15}\f$ |

For `gaussian_tail`, the log-density agrees with the normal limit within \f$8.17 \times 10^{-14}\f$ absolute (test bound \f$10^{-10}\f$) and the cumulative function of the quantile agrees with \f$p\f$ for \f$p\f$ from \f$10^{-8}\f$ to \f$1 - 10^{-8}\f$ within \f$2.5 \times 10^{-15}\f$ relative (test bound \f$10^{-10}\f$ relative). On the RTX 4070 SUPER the sum of the components of each order on the host against the device agrees within \f$10^{-15}\f$ at order 1 and \f$1.7 \times 10^{-16}\f$ to \f$1.01 \times 10^{-15}\f$ at the others for `gaussian_tail`, and within \f$1.2 \times 10^{-17}\f$ to \f$7.0 \times 10^{-16}\f$ over the orders 1 to 6 for `student_tail` and `logistic_tail` (test bound \f$2 \times 10^{-15}\f$ in both).

The closed \f$Z\f$ of each law agrees with the 400-bit quadrature of the definition \f$Z = 2\int_0^\infty e^{-q(u)}\,\mathrm{d}u\f$ to \f$1.1 \times 10^{-48}\f$ over all 36 cases, which also checks that each density integrates to one. On the inner pieces the two laws equal `mcp_penalty` and `scad_penalty` bit for bit in the value for every tail, and within \f$2\varepsilon\f$ of \f$\max(|\mathrm{value}|, 1)\f$ in the derivatives (test bound \f$8\varepsilon\f$, where \f$\varepsilon\f$ is double-precision machine epsilon); the derivatives in the tail's own parameters are zero there.

The log-density bounds are in ulp of the largest component of each order at the point, as the test defines them. For `gaussian_tail`, the figure \f$2.46\f$ ulp refers to \f$\max(\lvert \log F \rvert, 1)\f$; in ulp of \f$\log F\f$ itself the same rows reach \f$437\f$ ulp, which is the conditioning of a Gaussian tail, where the argument of \f$\Phi\f$ carries one ulp and \f$\log\Phi\f$ has slope \f$-z\f$ in its argument, \f$z\f$ the standardized distance beyond the knot. There is no closed quantile for this law with any tail: the \ref distrib_generic "generic route" serves it. The degrees of freedom \f$\nu\f$ of `student_tail` are weakly identified when only the tail informs them, so they will often be held fixed; the law admits them as a free parameter.

For `expected_info()`, `tools/reference/distrib_mcp_scad_info.py` generates \f$1200\f$ reference values at \f$200\f$ bits in mpmath (mpmath) over \f$100\f$ settings covering both the MCP and SCAD laws, all three tails, five pairs of law parameters, and three or four tail settings (`tests/distrib/test_mcp_scad_info.cpp`). The reference comes from the definition rather than from the closed forms: the location from the quadrature of \f$\mathbb{E}[q'(u)^2]\f$ and the smooth block from the quadrature of \f$\mathbb{E}[q_a q_b]\f$ less the product of the first derivatives of \f$\log Z\f$. Evaluated at \f$200\f$ bits, the closed assembly agrees with that reference to \f$2.0 \times 10^{-41}\f$. In double precision, measured as in \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured" and scaled by the largest component of the matrix, the worst error over the grid is \f$58.7\f$ ulp (test bound \f$90\f$ ulp), at the SCAD law with `logistic_tail` at \f$\lambda = 0.2\f$, \f$a = 1.4\f$ and \f$s = 6\f$, where the condition number of the assembly (the sum of the moduli of its terms over the modulus of the result) is \f$7.4\f$. That number stays between \f$1\f$ and \f$9\f$ over every setting and is the same for the three tails, so no term cancels and what is left is the rounding of the terms themselves. The generic quadrature, measured against the same reference, reaches \f$3.84\f$ ulp on gcc and \f$4.14\f$ on CUDA, so what the closed form buys is speed and not accuracy; both figures are below \f$10^{-14}\f$ relative. The constant of `student_tail` is read through the half-step log-gamma ratio of \ref stirling "Stirling's error", without which the matrix measured \f$706\f$ ulp at \f$\nu = 250\f$ (\ref distrib_properizing_tail "the properizing tails page"). Every off-diagonal location component \f$I_{\mu\theta}\f$ is checked to be an exact zero. Across the crossing \f$w = 1\f$, `dawson_moments` is continuous to \f$2.46 \times 10^{-15}\f$ relative over consecutive doubles of \f$\lambda\f$ at five values of \f$p\f$ (test bound \f$10^{-14}\f$). Against the generic score-form integral, `expected_info()` agrees within \f$1.3 \times 10^{-14}\f$ relative to the largest component (test bound \f$5 \times 10^{-14}\f$). Inside a Kokkos kernel the host and the device agree within \f$1.65\f$ ulp of the largest component over six law and tail pairs (test bound \f$16\f$ ulp), and the closed form measures the same \f$58.7\f$ ulp on the two builds.

For `expected_info_dk<K>()` at orders one to four (`tests/distrib/test_mcp_scad_info.cpp`), the orders are checked three independent ways because only order zero has 200-bit references. Differences are measured relative to the scale of the tensor, as \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured" defines it. Against five-point differences of the order below, the worst relative error over both laws and all three tails is \f$3.15 \times 10^{-11}\f$, \f$2.02 \times 10^{-10}\f$, \f$6.99 \times 10^{-10}\f$, and \f$1.29 \times 10^{-9}\f$ at orders one to four, chaining down to order zero against the references. Against the generic quadrature at orders one and two, the worst difference is \f$1.05 \times 10^{-15}\f$ relative over \f$474\f$ components. Inside a Kokkos kernel at order four, host and device agree within \f$0\f$ ulp on the OpenMP build and \f$6.69\f$ ulp on the CUDA build. The tables of the inner moments agree with five-point differences within \f$9.6 \times 10^{-10}\f$ relative.

For `prox_y()`, `tools/reference/distrib_mcp_scad_prox.py` generates \f$12243\f$ reference rows at \f$200\f$ bits in mpmath (mpmath) across both the MCP and SCAD laws and all three tails (`tests/distrib/test_mcp_scad_tails.cpp`). The grid spans \f$\lambda\f$, the second shape (\f$\gamma\f$ or \f$a - 1\f$), \f$U\f$ across the knots and thresholds out to \f$10000\f$ times the last knot, and \f$t\f$ through the convex, flat and concave regimes. On the \f$12075\f$ rows with a well-separated minimum, the computed minimizer agrees with the reference within \f$13\f$ ulp at worst (test bound \f$20\f$ ulp), occurring at the SCAD knot with a step of \f$20\f$. The remaining \f$168\f$ rows are the flat case, where the minimizer is an interval; no reference row has a relative objective gap between \f$0\f$ and \f$4.13 \times 10^{-8}\f$, so a flat optimum is never mistaken for a near tie. On every row, the objective \f$g\f$ at the computed point never exceeds the objective at the reference point by more than \f$3.95 \times 10^{-16}\f$ relative (test bound \f$64\f$ ulp of \f$\max(\lvert g \rvert, 1)\f$), so the winning branch is never missed. Where the minimizer falls strictly inside a piece, the objective derivative \f$t\,q'(x) + x - U\f$ vanishes to \f$7.01 \times 10^{-16}\f$ relative over \f$369\f$ points (test bound \f$10^{-12}\f$).

The references and the derivative checks are described in `docs/numerics.md`.

## Kokkos usage

```cpp
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/mcp.hpp>

void eval_mcp(Kokkos::View<const double*> y, Kokkos::View<double*> out,
              double mu, double lambda, double gamma, double tail_scale, double tail_df) {
  const laplace::distrib::mcp_distrib<laplace::distrib::student_tail> d{
      mu, lambda, gamma, tail_scale, tail_df};
  Kokkos::parallel_for("mcp_eval", y.extent(0), KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    out(i) = d.logpdf(y(i)) + d.logpdf_d1(y(i))[0] + d.cdf(y(i));
  });
}
```

## Members

Struct template \ref laplace::distrib::mcp_distrib "mcp_distrib" (default `Tail = student_tail`) has the structure of \ref distrib_core "a univariate distribution" with `n_params = 3 + Tail::n_tail_params`, which is 4 with `gaussian_tail` or `logistic_tail` and 5 with `student_tail`. Its fields are `mu`, `lambda`, `gamma` and `tail`. Its members are `kind`, `n_params`, `y_regularity`, `params`, `logpdf()`, `prox_y()`, `logpdf_d1()` to `logpdf_d6()`, `logpdf_dk()`, `logpdf_dy_dk()`, `expected_info()`, `expected_info_d1()` to `expected_info_d4()`, `expected_info_dk()`, `cdf()`, `ccdf()`, `log_cdf()`, `log_ccdf()`, `support_lower()`, `support_upper()`, `center()`, `scale()`, `param_kinks()`, `y_kinks()` and `start_value()`. The member `scale()` returns \f$1/\lambda\f$ plus `tail.scale_hint()`, a rough spread of the law. The member `start_value()` takes the sample median as \f$\mu\f$, the reciprocal of the mean absolute deviation about the median as \f$\lambda\f$, the value 3 as \f$\gamma\f$ and `tail.start_value(summary)` for the tail (\ref distrib_properizing_tail "Properizing tails", \ref distrib_start "Starting values").

